MVP IMPLEMENTATION ROADMAP
=========================

Stage 1 — Define MVP requirements and acceptance criteria
---------------------------------------------------------
Status: DONE
Tasks:
- Confirm RSS/public URL intake, languages, event categories and initial
  country catalog with the project owner.
- Define analyst/reviewer/reader/admin roles and internal report audience.
- Document retention/deletion, privacy and lawful-collection constraints.
- Publish measurable acceptance criteria and explicit MVP non-goals.
Done when:
- Confirmed requirements, constraints and non-goals are documented in
  docs/MVP_REQUIREMENTS.md and aligned with the MVP configuration.
- Acceptance criteria are observable and testable without subjective claims
  such as "the system verifies truth".

Stage 2 — Select and establish the minimal runtime architecture
--------------------------------------------------------------
Status: DONE
Tasks:
- Select Python 3.12+ and the minimal setuptools src-layout package.
- Select a CLI-first workflow; defer REST API until a demonstrated requirement.
- Implement configuration validation and CNVS_CONFIG_DIR/CNVS_LOG_LEVEL
  environment handling.
- Document editable installation, setup, test and CLI commands.
- Update CI to install the project and exercise its CLI configuration check.
Done when:
- A clean checkout can be set up using docs/DEVELOPMENT.md and the configured
  runtime can validate the reviewed MVP catalog.
- No secrets or machine-specific configuration are required in source control.

Stage 3 — Implement canonical records and persistent storage
------------------------------------------------------------
Status: DONE
Tasks:
- Implement typed event, source, claim, evidence and assessment snapshot models.
- Validate persisted records against explicit versioned JSON Schemas.
- Implement checksummed SQLite migrations, canonical record persistence and
  append-only revision history.
- Preserve raw source snapshots by SHA-256, verify content integrity and reject
  in-place updates/deletes.
- Capture the exact revisions linked to each assessment for reconstruction.
Done when:
- Valid records can be saved and retrieved as typed objects; malformed or
  cross-event references are rejected with actionable errors.
- A stored assessment can be reconstructed with the exact event/source/claim/
  evidence revisions used when it was recorded.
- Updates append revisions; prior record payloads and raw snapshots cannot be
  modified or deleted through the database API.

Stage 4 — Build source registry and collection policy
-----------------------------------------------------
Status: IMPLEMENTED; approval of any live source set remains pending
Tasks:
- Add a validated analyst-managed YAML registry with publisher, source class,
  country, language, public access method, event relevance, review state and
  bounded collection/retention constraints.
- Keep the checked-in example synthetic, pending and disabled; do not imply
  that a live event-relevant source set has been reviewed or endorsed.
- Implement bounded RSS/URL intake with timestamps, origin identifiers,
  redirect/public-address checks and `robots.txt` respect.
- Persist immutable collection results, source-registry snapshots, optional
  content snapshots, explicit failures/access blocks and sequential manual
  retries.
Done when:
- An analyst can configure and approve a public RSS/URL source, collect it,
  and inspect every success, failure, access restriction and retry.
- Collected content is retained only when the source-specific constraints allow
  it, with immutable source metadata and origin provenance.
- Automated tests exercise synthetic RSS/URL responses and access restrictions.
  The initial real-world source set remains unpopulated until an event is
  selected and the responsible analyst completes source/legal review.
Validation: 49 tests pass on Python 3.12 (installed wheel) and Python 3.14
(workspace environment). The wheel includes both database migrations; its CLI
validated configuration, source registry and SQLite migration status.

Stage 5 — Normalize, identify language and deduplicate sources
--------------------------------------------------------------
Status: TODO
Tasks:
- Preserve original text alongside normalized text.
- Detect or record language and retain translation provenance.
- Normalize timestamps, URLs and publisher metadata without losing original
  values.
- Detect exact duplicates and likely syndication/republication relationships.
- Keep uncertain duplicate and dependency decisions reviewable.
Done when:
- Repeated copies are linked to their identifiable origin where possible.
- Duplicate detection never turns dependent copies into independent evidence.
- Original source text and timestamps remain available.

Stage 6 — Resolve events and build timelines
--------------------------------------------
Status: TODO
Tasks:
- Implement event creation and analyst-assisted event matching.
- Link incoming sources to candidate events with traceable rationale.
- Preserve event time, publication time and collection time independently.
- Provide an editable chronological timeline.
Done when:
- An analyst can inspect, correct and audit event/source links and chronology.
- Ambiguous event matches remain unresolved rather than being silently merged.

Stage 7 — Extract claims and attribution as derived records
----------------------------------------------------------
Status: TODO
Tasks:
- Extract candidate claims, subjects, predicates, objects, modality and
  attribution from source material.
- Link each extracted claim to its exact source and relevant text span where
  available.
- Mark automated output as derived and distinguish it from analyst-reviewed
  records.
- Add correction, rejection and review status for candidate extractions.
- Treat source text as untrusted input; enforce prompt-injection safeguards if
  language models are used.
Done when:
- Claims are never detached from their source.
- Analyst review can correct or reject extracted claims.
- No automated extraction is represented as primary evidence or confirmed fact.

Stage 8 — Implement provenance graph and independence groups
------------------------------------------------------------
Status: TODO
Tasks:
- Represent source-to-source citation, quotation, syndication and derivation
  links.
- Record the earliest identifiable origin and uncertainty about origin.
- Assign and review evidence independence groups.
- Expose the rationale and supporting links behind independence decisions.
Done when:
- Analysts can trace a claim back through its known source chain.
- Repeated reports from one origin are not counted as independent confirmation.
- Unknown or uncertain source relationships are explicitly represented.

Stage 9 — Record evidence and contradictions
--------------------------------------------
Status: TODO
Tasks:
- Implement evidence records for direct observations and relevant primary
  material, including collection time, event time, directness and verification
  state.
- Link evidence to claims as supporting, contradicting or not directly
  relevant.
- Record analyst notes and verification decisions with provenance.
- Represent missing, conflicting and insufficient evidence explicitly.
Done when:
- Each evidence item has traceable origin and verification status.
- Claim support and contradiction are inspectable and not inferred from article
  volume alone.

Stage 10 — Build the national information matrix
------------------------------------------------
Status: TODO
Tasks:
- Select country coverage dynamically for each event from a reviewed country
  and language configuration.
- Summarize reporting, institutional statements, primary evidence, dominant
  frame, attribution and confidence by country.
- Show coverage gaps and source dependencies alongside comparisons.
Done when:
- The MVP supports a reviewed initial set of 10–15 countries.
- Country comparisons distinguish reporting volume from independent evidence
  and do not treat a country as inherently correct.

Stage 11 — Produce analyst-reviewed reports
-------------------------------------------
Status: TODO
Tasks:
- Generate Markdown and HTML reports from linked, versioned records.
- Separate FACT, CLAIM, ASSESSMENT, CONFIDENCE and GAP sections.
- Show occurrence, method and attribution confidence separately.
- Include source provenance, independence groups, contradictions, timeline and
  information gaps.
- Require documented human review before high-impact publication.
Done when:
- A reviewed event can be exported as a reproducible report with linked sources
  and evidence.
- Unresolved attribution and uncertainty are preserved in rendered output.

Stage 12 — Operational quality, security and reliability
---------------------------------------------------------
Status: TODO
Tasks:
- Add tests for ingestion, normalization, deduplication, extraction, provenance,
  evidence linking, reporting and failure paths.
- Add structured logs and basic metrics for collection and processing failures.
- Implement timeouts, bounded retries and visible partial-failure reporting.
- Validate inputs; protect credentials; isolate risky document parsing.
- Review data access, retention, backups and audit requirements.
- Enforce the architecture quality gates for provenance, independence,
  evidence, attribution, contradiction, time, uncertainty and human review.
Done when:
- Failure of a source or external provider is visible and does not silently
  produce a success-shaped result.
- Critical analytical invariants are covered by automated tests and documented
  analyst procedures.
- Security and privacy review is complete for the intended MVP deployment.

Stage 13 — MVP release readiness
-------------------------------
Status: TODO
Tasks:
- Run an end-to-end exercise on synthetic or explicitly authorized material.
- Verify the complete RSS/URL-to-reviewed-report workflow.
- Test the documented setup from a clean environment and run CI.
- Review schemas, configuration, retention policy and analyst guide.
- Document known limitations, deployment instructions and rollback/recovery.
Done when:
- Every capability in the MVP scope is implemented and demonstrated.
- The acceptance criteria from Stage 1 pass.
- Human reviewers can trace report statements to claims, sources and evidence.
- Release notes clearly state limitations; no deferred capability is claimed.


MVP EXIT CHECKLIST
==================

[ ] RSS and URL collection works for the approved source set.
[ ] Collection scope, country/language catalog and event categories match
    docs/MVP_REQUIREMENTS.md.
[ ] Raw collected material and provenance are retained.
[ ] Initial multilingual source workflow is supported.
[ ] Source duplicates and known syndication are linked.
[ ] Claims and attribution are extracted or entered and linked to sources.
[ ] Analysts can inspect and correct candidate claims.
[ ] Basic source graph and evidence independence groups are available.
[ ] Evidence, contradictions and unresolved gaps are represented.
[ ] Event timeline is available with distinct event/publication/collection times.
[ ] National information matrix supports the selected 10–15 countries.
[ ] Markdown and HTML reports are generated from versioned records.
[ ] High-impact reports require and record human review.
[ ] Tests, security safeguards, documented setup and CI pass.
[ ] No repetition is reported as independent confirmation.
[ ] Occurrence, method and attribution remain separate confidence dimensions.


EXPLICITLY DEFERRED BEYOND MVP
==============================

- Full EU and European NATO coverage and broader strategic-neighborhood
  expansion.
- Automated narrative clustering and advanced contradiction detection.
- Advanced graph analytics, semantic search and analyst dashboard.
- Near-real-time monitoring and specialized satellite/AIS integrations.
- Model ensembles, calibrated uncertainty, anomaly detection and early warning.
- GEWS/Sentinel integrations and advanced collaborative analyst workflows.

These capabilities belong to later phases in ARCHITECTURE.md and must not be
treated as MVP-complete by default.
